A||N||14||85Josh||Duke Cam Gear Change?||||8||
Z||000000||85Josh||05-17-2001||06:57 PM||||My 85 duke just quit running today. Looked in the oil fill hole, no rockerarm movement when the engine was cranking. I don't want to drop the engine right now. Does anyone here know how to change the gears without pulling the engine? I'm really stuck, with no place to work but my driveway, and the trailer park manager is trying to get me in trouble for that. TIA<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||8
Z||000001||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-17-2001||09:00 PM||||There was a post in the past about this.  It described partially dropping the cradle.  <P>Some members tried it and posted that the method was probably easier that removing the cradle.  <P>It looked to me like there were some risks as debris could get into the oil pan.  <P>This is always the dilemma.  <P>If you are fixing the timing gears, what about those noisey lifters and the pushrods, lifters, vavle springs?<P>If you do all that, what about the valve job?<P>If you do a top end rebuild, then what about the bottom end?<P>If your timing gear is shot, you have to do at least half of the work to pull the engine to fix it.  Why not do all the work?  <P>Pros:<BR>1.  You won't have to worry about doing this partial engine repair and then going back a month later to the whole job anyway.<BR>2.  If the timing gear failed, then what condition is the rest of the engine in?  Probably just as worn.<BR>3.  A rebuilt engine makes more power and gets better gas mileage than an old one.<P>Cons:<P>1.  It will cost more money to do the whole job<BR>2.  It will take more time to do the whole job<P><BR>You have two choices:<P>1.  Gamble:  Fix the timing gear and hope that you are one of the "Lucky" ones<BR>2.  Take a deep breath and accept that doing the whole job now will mean that you won't be doing it again later<P>I think, in the end, the rebuild (or rebuilt replacement engine) is the better baragain.<P>Maybe this last question will help you decide:<P>Do you want to drive the car for the next six months or the next six years?||||reg||1
Z||000002||drednot||05-17-2001||09:01 PM||||option 3-<BR>Junk the car, get a better one!||||reg||1
Z||000003||drednot||05-17-2001||09:02 PM||||option 3-<BR>Junk the car, get a better one!||||reg||10
Z||000004||85Josh||05-17-2001||09:12 PM||||Iron Duke, I'd like to keep the car forever, but space and money are my biggest problems right now. I can't pull the engine in the space I have without getting in serious trouble, and I can't afford to pay someone else to fix it. I also can't afford to drive my 8mpg van for more than a week, as I drive approx. 400 miles to work and back each week. My ultimate intention is to get another engine, rebuild it, then take a weeks vacation, live in a buddy's garage, and do it right. Just can't do that right now. Meantime, can anyone point me towards the info I need, as I didn't find anything with my search parameters(WHEW, That was long! I feel better now!)||||reg||1
Z||000005||85Josh||05-17-2001||09:14 PM||||HELL NO, drednot, I love Lil' Bit! Besides, I don't have any money to speak of right now.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||05-17-2001||10:00 PM||||The Online Service guide has the info on changing the timing gear at <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero_osg" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero_osg</A> <P>Go to the Engine/Drivetrain section and then find the Timing Gear Replacement.<P>Good luck...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
