Z||000008||MODIFIE||05-17-2001||11:34 PM||||man i see this on the web some place and ever time i see someone like your self asking i thank i know i should have saved that so i am going to add a page to my site called special parts replacements and start saving this stuff i don't have that page yet byt i will, but look around if you like <A HREF="http://www.dukemods.50megs.com/PAGE1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dukemods.50megs.com/PAGE1.html</A> ||||reg||8
Z||000009||Brucepts||05-18-2001||12:35 AM||||I did one in the car, you have to remove the cover and bust whats left of the old one off. This will leave the steel ring on the shaft. It will have to be cut off with a die grinder and cutoff wheel. One slit is all that is needed since it is a press fit. (Cover everything with rags so the dirt doesn't go everywhere). This was the hard part!! Once you have the old one off you need to tap the end of the cam with threads so you can use a bolt to press the new one on. The gear needs to be heated so it will slip on without alot of pressure since you will only be using the threads of the bolt to press it on the cam ::whew:: hopes this all makes sense so far? A GM mechanic told me this trick, its how they make the rate schedule at the shop  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> It worked for me  thats all I can say . . . won't do it that way next time though. Metal gear next time when I do the whole motor.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cozmo||05-18-2001||08:29 AM||||I just did this on my duke...<BR>Partially dropped the cradle, pulled out the cam and took it to the machine shop for replacement.  Lots of people have suggested doing it in place, but I was a little uncomfortable with the methods involved. It was a weekend job, not too tough just tedious. Cost me $60 for the METAL gear set (DON'T get the fiber ones) and that included the machine shop pressing on the new gear.  Drop the oil pan to get all the old crap out, if the rest of the engine is OK you should be good to go.<BR>C02M0||||reg||1
Z||000011||erikred||05-18-2001||09:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Brucepts:<BR><B> A GM mechanic told me this trick, its how they make the rate schedule at the shop   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> It worked for me  thats all I can say . . . </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got the same advice from my local dealer shop, only we chisled of the metal ring instead of cutting it off. cutting it off seems like it would be a little trickier and messier. After doing it this way a couple times to different vehicles, there is no way I would do it the conventional way (unless I wanted an excuse to change to a better cam). It is much faster. You will need to clean out the oil pan though, but that can be done with the engine in also.||||reg||14
Z||000012||85Josh||05-18-2001||12:18 PM||||Thanks, guys. I guess this will have to wait until next week, I don't work from Wed. till Tues., thanks to creative scheduling and Memorial day. I'll keep y'all posted.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000013||Songman||05-19-2001||01:08 PM||||I'm at the same place with my 88 coupe, Josh. That is why it has been sitting out here in my driveway since the day after Christmas. I'm going to check out some of the referred pages and see what they have to say. <P>I like the idea of just removing the cam. For those that did it that way, any special things I need to do to make sure I get it back in the right way?<P>Good luck, Josh.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
